    "summary": "Turn 26 reviewed the applied-state artifact and rejected its attribution rather than letting an out-of-scope observation be counted as confirmation.\n\nState on entry. The artifact 05dc6a3632954371911663108e7e18e8 (executed as 2f186bc17e13432fae3b653b42e46941) reported violated with the oracle correspondence unreviewed. Its retained stream reproduces the observation: applied_state_divergence with peer_value 04 against recoverer_value and third_value (void), applied_match false, so the disagreement in committed records does reach the state machines.\n\nThe issue (checker_correspondence, revision_needed, challenged component: scope). The accepted claim C1-recovery-adopts-committed-vector requires that a reply reporting the instance COMMITTED be adopted, and its scope list explicitly excludes \"execution of the instance and client-visible replies\". The added property asserts that after each replica applies the instance it holds committed the state machines agree on the instance's key, which is a consequence of the requirement and sits in that excluded region. Confirming it under C1 would treat an independent limit as a confirmation gate, so the item asks for the property to be dropped from this artifact or the proposition to be fixed as its own obligation whose scope includes applying the instance to the state machine. The review also records what is sound: the harness applies each record with Exec.executeCommand and probes the store with a GET, the three values are printed and read from the replicated store rather than derived from a changing context, and the instance identity is shared with the prerequisite reply, so the failure is not a correlation artefact. Counterevidence cites the claim's excluded list and the reproducible event line, and the limitations keep the observation available as construction knowledge for a new obligation, note that even the substituted execution skips the production loop, watermarks and the recovery timeout, and confirm